) ÷ 2 = 62) and Class Y = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 55, 58, 60, 65, 68, 68, 75, 80, 82, 84, 86, 88, 90, 95, 100} (median (68 + 68) ÷ 2 = 68); pooled, the sorted list’s 16th and 17th values are 65 and 68, so the pooled median is 66.5 — higher than Class X’s 62. (An example where the pooled median exceeds the LOWER class median is the strongest valid reading of the question; exceeding BOTH is mathematically impossible.) The warning: a class median is a summary of that class only. It cannot be combined with another class’s median to predict the cohort median — the full data are needed; and quoting one class’s median as if it described the school (or comparing schools by it) can mislead.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark: concludes 70 is impossible (at least 16 of 32 marks ≤ 68). 1 mark: any correct own example showing the pooled median differing from the class medians (accept ‘higher than one of the class medians’; award full credit for any correctly computed example). 1 mark: the warning — class medians describe that class only; pooled median needs the raw data; quoting them for the whole cohort misleads.\\n\\nAuthor’s note — The question as printed (‘higher than both class medians’) is mathematically impossible for any pooling of raw data; the model answer corrects this and rewards the strongest valid reasoning. Flagged in the build report.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — any correctly worked own example; warning phrased as ‘you cannot average two medians’ or ‘medians do not combine’.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — First, the teacher’s 70 is IMPOSSIBLE for these data. At least 6 of X’s 12 marks are ≤ 62 (so ≤ 68) and at least 10 of Y’s 20 marks are ≤ 68: that is at least 16 of the 32 pooled marks ≤ 68, so the 16th pooled mark is ≤ 68 and the pooled median — the mean of the 16th and 17th marks — cannot exceed 68. Any value of 70 must come from an arithmetic slip or from pooling the wrong list. What pooled data CAN do is shift the median away from either class median: in general the 