he mean of the 16th and 17th marks — cannot exceed 68. Any value of 70 must come from an arithmetic slip or from pooling the wrong list. What pooled data CAN do is shift the median away from either class median: in general the pooled median always lies between the two class medians, e.g. Class X = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 64, 70, 72, 74, 76, 78} (median (60 + 64) ÷ 2 = 62) and Class Y = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 55, 58, 60, 65, 68, 68, 75, 80, 82, 84, 86, 88, 90, 95, 100} (median (68 + 68) ÷ 2 = 68); pooled, the sorted list’s 16th and 17th values are 65 and 68, so the pooled median is 66.5 — higher than Class X’s 62. (An example where the pooled median exceeds the LOWER class median is the strongest valid reading of the question; exceeding BOTH is mathematically impossible.) The warning: a class median is a summary of that class only. It cannot be combined with another class’s median to predict the cohort median — the full data are needed; and quoting one class’s median as if it described the school (or comparing schools by it) can mislead.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark: concludes 70 is impossible (at least 16 of 32 marks ≤ 68). 1 mark: any correct own example showing the pooled median differing from the class medians (accept ‘higher than one of the class medians’; award full credit for any correctly computed example). 1 mark: the warning — class medians describe that class only; pooled median needs the raw data; quoting them for the whole cohort misleads.\\n\\nAuthor’s note — The question as printed (‘higher than both class medians’) is mathematically impossible for any pooling of raw data; the model answer corrects this and rewards the strongest valid reasoning. Flagged in the build report.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — any correctly worked own example; warning phrased as ‘you cannot average two medians’ or ‘medians do not combine’."},{"n":62,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The monthly incomes, in thousands of FCFA, of seven market traders are 18, 20, 22, 24, 26, 30 and 120. A journalist writes that the traders earn about 37 thousand FCFA a month.\\n\\na Work out the measure the journalist used, find the median, and explain why the median describes these traders more honestly than the mean. (2)","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The monthly incomes, in thousands of FCFA, of seven market traders are 18, 20, 22, 24, 26, 30 and 120. A journalist writes that the traders earn about 37 thousand FCFA a month."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a Work out the measure the journalist used, find the median, and explain why the median describes these traders more honestly than the mean. (2)"}]}]},"e":"Model answer — Sum = 18 + 20 + 22 + 24 + 26 + 30 + 120 = 260; mean = 260 ÷ 7 ≈ 37.14 thousand FCFA — the measure the journalist used. Sorted: 18, 20, 22, 24, 26, 30, 120; median = 4th value = 24 thousand FCFA. The income 120 is a strong outlier: it pulls the mean far above what six of the seven traders earn (30 or less). The median is not moved by the outlier, so it describes a typical trader far more honestly.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the mean ≈ 37.14 (±0.01) or for identifying it as the measure used with correct working. 1 mark for the median 24 with an explanation referencing the outlier/skew. An answer giving the median without explanation scores 0 for the explanatory element.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — mean given as ≈37.1 or 37; ‘the average they used was the mean’; median 24 000? No — the question asks in thousands of FCFA: 24 (i.e. 24 000 FCFA).","m":2,"df":"easy","tp":"Measures of Central Tendency","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":62,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Add them all, then look at the gap between mean and the 4th value.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a1","label":"(a) journalist’s measure","prompt":"Work out the measure the journalist used (the mean), to 2 decimal places.","kind":"num