ntent":[{"type":"text","text":"Two bakeries in Porto-Novo both record the number of loaves they sell each hour. Bakery P has a mean of 30 loaves per hour and a standard deviation of 4; Bakery Q has a mean of 30 loaves per hour and a standard deviation of 12."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a Say which bakery has the more consistent sales, and explain what a standard deviation of 12 says about Bakery Q''s trading. (2)"}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Bakery P is more consistent: identical mean (30) but σ = 4 vs Q’s 12 — P’s hourly sales stay close to 30, while Q’s swing widely. A standard deviation of 12 means Q’s hourly sales deviate from 30 by about 12 loaves on average — roughly speaking many hours sell far below or far above 30 (within one σ, about 18 to 42 if sales are normal), so Q’s trading is erratic and hard to plan stock for, even though the two bakeries average the same.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark: Bakery P with the smaller-σ reason. 1 mark: σ = 12 = typical deviation from the mean; sales highly variable/erratic (accept any correct interpretation, e.g. range within one σ, unpredictability). Answers listing only a formula score 0 for the second mark.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘P varies less’; σ interpreted as average distance of sales from 30 loaves.","m":2,"df":"easy","tp":"Standard Deviation","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":63,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Same centre — compare the spread; then say what 12 loaves means per hour.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a1","label":"(a) more consistent","prompt":"Say which bakery has the more consistent sales, and why.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Same centre, different spread — smaller σ means more consistent.","Compare the two spreads — which is smaller, and what does that say about consistency?"],"accepted":["Bakery P — its standard deviation is smaller (4 vs 12), so sales cluster more tightly around the mean","P; σ = 4 < σ = 12 means less variability in hourly sales"],"keywords":[["p"],["smaller","less","lower","tighter","4"],["consistent","stable","spread","vary","variability"]],"minKeywordGroups":3},{"id":"a2","label":"(a) meaning of σ = 12","prompt":"Explain what a standard deviation of 12 says about Bakery Q''s trading.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["σ is the typical distance of hourly sales from the mean.","Sales fluctuate a lot: roughly hours range widely (about 18–42 within one σ if normal)."],"accepted":["Q’s hourly sales typically deviate from 30 by about 12 loaves — sales are erratic/unpredictable, with some very busy and some very quiet hours","Q fluctuates heavily around 30 loaves per hour (roughly 18 to 42 in a typical hour, and wider beyond that); trading is far less predictable than P’s"],"keywords":[["12","deviate","from the mean","around 30","spread","fluctuat","vary","range","erratic","unpredictable","swing"]],"minKeywordGroups":1}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) Bakery P is more consistent: identical mean (30) but σ = 4 vs Q’s 12 — P’s hourly sales stay close to 30, while Q’s swing widely. A standard deviation of 12 means Q’s hourly sales deviate from 30 by about 12 loaves on average — roughly speaking many hours sell far below or far above 30 (within one σ, about 18 to 42 if sales are normal), so Q’s trading is erratic and hard to plan stock for, even though the two bakeries average the same.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark: Bakery P with the smaller-σ reason. 1 mark: σ = 12 = typical deviation from the mean; sales highly variable/erratic (accept any correct interpretation, e.g. range within one σ, unpredictability). Answers listing only a formula score 0 for the second mark.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘P varies less’; σ interpreted as average distance of sales from 30 loaves.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) Bakery P is more consistent: identical mean (30) but σ = 4 vs Q’s 12 — P’s hourly sales stay close to 30, while Q’s swing widely. A standard deviation of 12 means Q’s hourly sales deviate from 30 by about 12 loaves on average — roughly speaking many hours sell far below or far above 30 (within one σ, about 18 to 42 if sales are normal), so Q’s trading is erratic and hard to plan stock for, even though the two bakeries average the same.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark: Bakery P with the smaller-σ reason. 1 mark: σ = 12 = typical deviation from the mean; sales highly variable/erratic (accept any correct interpretation, e.g. range within one σ, unpredictability). Answers listing only a formula score 0 for the second mark.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘P varies less’; σ interpreted as average distance of sales from 30 loaves.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) Bakery P is more consistent: identical mean (30) but σ = 4 vs Q’s 12 — P’s hourly sales stay close to 30, while Q’s swing widely. A standard deviation of 12 means Q’s hourly sales deviate from 30 by about 12 loaves on average — roughly speaking many hours sell far below or far above 30 (within one σ, about 18 to 42 if sales are normal), so Q’s trading is erratic and hard to plan stock for, even though the two bakeries average the same.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark: Bakery P with the smaller-σ reason. 1 mark: σ = 12 = typical deviation from the mean; sales highly variable/erratic (accept any correct interpretation, e.g. range within one σ, unpredictability). Answers listing only a formula score 0 for the second mark.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘P varies less’; σ interpreted as average distance of sales from 30 loaves."}]'::jsonb) as x
